---
title: Sync to External Platforms
date: 2026-06-16
description: How to publish .hd documentation to external platforms like Nextra, Docusaurus, and GitBook using the hd Claude plugin and the /hd:sync slash command.
---

<h1>Sync to External Platforms</h1>

<p>HD is the source format. To deliver documentation to readers you publish it to an external platform — Nextra, Docusaurus, GitBook, and so on. The <code>hd</code> Claude plugin handles this with a single slash command, <code>/hd:sync</code>, driven by a small per-directory config file.</p>

<h2>The model</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>One source directory</strong> — the folder of <code>.hd</code> files you want to publish.</li>
  <li><strong>One config file</strong> — <code>hd-sync.json</code>, placed inside that directory. It defines what counts as part of the export and where it goes.</li>
  <li><strong>One or more destinations</strong> — each destination is a publishing target with its own platform type (e.g., Nextra 4) and its own output location. A single source can publish to many destinations at once.</li>
  <li><strong>The slash command</strong> — <code>/hd:sync</code> discovers configs in the codebase, lets you pick a destination, and runs the conversion.</li>
</ul>

<p>Sync is one-way: HD → target. Files in the target are generated artifacts and will be overwritten on the next run.</p>

<h2>Setting up a directory to sync</h2>

<h3>1. Create <code>hd-sync.json</code> in the source directory</h3>

<p>Place the file alongside the <code>.hd</code> files it controls. The config holds an array of destinations:</p>

<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "destinations": [
    {
      "name": "nextra 4",
      "type": "nextra4",
      "description": "Public docs site.",
      "path": "../../Docs/hd-docs",
      "contentDir": "src/content",
      "publicDir": "public",
      "assetMap": [
        { "from": "images", "to": "public/images" }
      ],
      "meta": {
        "index": { "title": "Overview", "theme": { "breadcrumb": false } },
        "getting-started": "Getting Started"
      }
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

<h3>2. Common destination fields</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Unique label within this config. Shown in the destination picker.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>type</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Converter key. Currently supported: <code>nextra4</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>path</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Path to the target project root. Resolved relative to the config file.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>no</td><td>One-line description shown in the destination picker.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>routePrefix</code></td><td>no</td><td>String prepended to internal link URLs and to the asset folder path. Required when multiple HD projects publish into the same target repo. See <a href="#multi-project">Sharing a target repo</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>deleteOrphans</code></td><td>no</td><td><code>true</code> | <code>false</code> | <code>"warn"</code> (default <code>"warn"</code>). Controls whether stale files in the target are removed, warned about, or ignored. See <a href="#orphans">Orphan handling</a>.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Additional fields are owned by each destination type. The <code>nextra4</code> type accepts <code>contentDir</code>, <code>publicDir</code>, <code>assetMap</code>, and <code>meta</code> — see the <code>hd-to-mdx</code> skill for the full reference.</p>

<h3>3. Multiple destinations</h3>

<p>To publish the same source to several platforms, add more entries to the <code>destinations</code> array:</p>

<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "destinations": [
    { "name": "nextra 4", "type": "nextra4", "path": "../../Docs/hd-nextra", "...": "..." },
    { "name": "docusaurus", "type": "docusaurus", "path": "../../Docs/hd-docusaurus", "...": "..." },
    { "name": "gitbook", "type": "gitbook", "path": "../../Docs/hd-gitbook", "...": "..." }
  ]
}</code></pre>

<p>Each destination is independent. Running the sync against one does not affect the others.</p>

<h2>Running a sync</h2>

<h3>The slash command</h3>

<p>From any working directory at or above the source directory, run:</p>

<pre><code>/hd:sync</code></pre>

<p>The command walks the working directory looking for <code>hd-sync.json</code> files (up to four levels deep, skipping <code>node_modules</code>, <code>dist</code>, <code>.git</code>, and similar). What happens next depends on what it finds:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Discovery result</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>No configs</td><td>Reports that none were found and exits.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One config, one destination</td><td>Runs without prompting.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One config, multiple destinations</td><td>Asks which destination.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Multiple configs</td><td>Asks which config, then which destination.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Direct CLI</h3>

<p>The plugin's underlying script is also runnable directly, which is useful for scripting or for CI:</p>

<pre><code>node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs                                  # list discovered configs
node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs --list                           # same, JSON
node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs --config &lt;path&gt; --dest "&lt;name&gt;"  # run a sync</code></pre>

<p>When using <code>--config</code> without <code>--dest</code>, the script will run the sync automatically only if the config has exactly one destination; otherwise it lists what's available and exits.</p>

<h2 id="orphans">Orphan handling</h2>

<p>An <em>orphan</em> is a file in the target with no corresponding source on this sync — usually because the HD file was renamed or deleted but the previous output is still sitting in the target.</p>

<p>The <code>deleteOrphans</code> field controls what the sync does with them:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Value</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>"warn"</code> (default)</td><td>Lists orphans in the sync output as warnings. Leaves them in place.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>true</code></td><td>Deletes orphan <code>.mdx</code> files from <code>contentDir</code>. If <code>routePrefix</code> is set, also deletes orphan asset folders from <code>publicDir/assets/&lt;routePrefix&gt;/</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>false</code></td><td>Silent — no warning, no deletion.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><strong>Safety property:</strong> deletion is always scoped to this project's own folders. The <code>.mdx</code> sweep only reads <code>contentDir</code>, which each project owns. The asset sweep only reads <code>publicDir/assets/&lt;routePrefix&gt;/</code>, which is unique per project. A sync cannot see, let alone delete, files belonging to another project — even when they share the same target repo.</p>

<p>If <code>deleteOrphans: true</code> is set without <code>routePrefix</code>, asset cleanup is skipped (with a warning explaining why), because <code>publicDir/assets/</code> would be a shared namespace. <code>.mdx</code> deletion still runs because <code>contentDir</code> is project-scoped either way.</p>

<h2 id="multi-project">Sharing a target repo across projects</h2>

<p>Multiple HD source directories can publish into the same Nextra target repo — for example, a product documentation site that aggregates docs from several internal codebases. To stay isolated, each project must:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Use its own <code>contentDir</code> sub-folder (e.g., <code>src/content/projA</code>, <code>src/content/projB</code>).</li>
  <li>Set <code>routePrefix</code> to match the sub-folder (e.g., <code>"projA"</code>).</li>
</ol>

<p>With both set, link and asset URLs are rewritten with the prefix, asset files are written to a per-project sub-folder under <code>publicDir/assets/&lt;routePrefix&gt;/</code>, and orphan deletion is bounded to that sub-folder. Two configs with different prefixes are physically incapable of touching each other's output.</p>

<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "destinations": [
    {
      "name": "nextra 4",
      "type": "nextra4",
      "path": "../../Docs/hd-docs",
      "contentDir": "src/content/projA",
      "routePrefix": "projA",
      "deleteOrphans": true
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

<p>Without <code>routePrefix</code>, internal <code>.hd</code> links rewrite to <code>/foo</code> (the repo root), which is correct only when the project's <code>contentDir</code> is <code>src/content</code> itself. If <code>contentDir</code> is a sub-folder and <code>routePrefix</code> is missing, internal links between docs in the same project will 404.</p>

<h2>What the sync does and doesn't do</h2>

<h3>Does</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Converts every <code>.hd</code> file in the source directory to the destination format.</li>
  <li>Rewrites internal <code>.hd</code> links to the platform's route style (with <code>routePrefix</code> applied if set).</li>
  <li>Copies assets per <code>assetMap</code> and per-doc asset folders.</li>
  <li>Generates the platform's sidebar or manifest file from the config (e.g., <code>_meta.js</code> for Nextra).</li>
  <li>Optionally deletes orphan output when <code>deleteOrphans: true</code> is set (see <a href="#orphans">Orphan handling</a>).</li>
  <li>Reports counts and warnings.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Doesn't</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Doesn't sync in reverse.</strong> Hand-edits in the target will not survive the next sync. If you need platform-specific customization, do it in the source HD file or upstream into the converter.</li>
  <li><strong>Doesn't delete files in the target unless asked.</strong> The default <code>deleteOrphans: "warn"</code> only flags orphans. Set <code>true</code> to actually remove them.</li>
  <li><strong>Doesn't look outside the source directory.</strong> Links in <code>.hd</code> files to files outside the source dir (for example, <code>../README.md</code> or another HD file at the project root) are flagged as warnings and left unchanged. They will resolve as broken links in the published site.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Warnings you may see</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Warning</th><th>What it means</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>external .hd reference (outside source dir)</code></td>
      <td>A link to a <code>.hd</code> file above the source directory.</td>
      <td>Move the file into the source dir, drop the link, or rewrite to an absolute URL.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>external repo link not rewritten</code></td>
      <td>A link to a non-<code>.hd</code> file above the source directory (e.g., a <code>.md</code> at the project root).</td>
      <td>Same options as above.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>asset source missing</code></td>
      <td>An <code>assetMap</code> entry pointed at a directory that doesn't exist.</td>
      <td>Fix the path in <code>hd-sync.json</code> or remove the entry.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>orphan in target (no HD source)</code></td>
      <td>A <code>.mdx</code> in the target's content directory has no corresponding <code>.hd</code> source.</td>
      <td>Delete manually, or set <code>deleteOrphans: true</code> on the destination to automate it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>orphan asset folder (no HD source)</code></td>
      <td>An asset folder under <code>publicDir/assets/&lt;routePrefix&gt;/</code> has no corresponding <code>.hd</code> source. Only emitted when <code>routePrefix</code> is set.</td>
      <td>Delete manually, or set <code>deleteOrphans: true</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>asset orphan cleanup skipped: no routePrefix set</code></td>
      <td>You set <code>deleteOrphans: true</code> but no <code>routePrefix</code>. Asset deletion was skipped to avoid accidentally deleting another project's files under a shared <code>publicDir/assets/</code>.</td>
      <td>Add <code>routePrefix</code>, or accept that assets won't be auto-cleaned.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>After a sync</h2>

<p>The sync produces source files for the external platform; it does not build the site itself. Run the platform's build to verify the output compiles and the pages render. For Nextra 4 that's <code>npm run build</code> in the target project — prerender is the only catch-all for MDX/JSX errors the converter can't predict.</p>

